\begin{Large}
        Exercise 1:\\
        Properties of sequences (40pts)
Let $\left\{\vec{x}_n\right\}$ and $\left\{\vec{y}_n\right\}$ be two sequences of points in $\mathbb{R}^m$, and let $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}$ be a real number. Suppose that $\left\{\vec{x}_n\right\}$ converges to a point $\vec{p}$, and $\left\{\vec{y}_n\right\}$ converges to a point $\vec{q}$. By giving a formal $\varepsilon-N$ proof, establish the following:\\
(10pts) The sequence $\left\{\lambda \vec{x}_n\right\}$ converges to $\lambda \vec{p}$,
Hint: consider $\lambda=0$ as a separate case.\\
(10pts) The sequence $\left\{\vec{z}_n=\vec{x}_n+\vec{y}_n\right\}$ converges to $\vec{p}+\vec{q}$.
(20pts) The sequence of real numbers $\left\{a_n=\left\langle\vec{x}_n, \vec{y}_n\right\rangle\right\}$ given by the inner product of $\vec{x}_n$ with $\vec{y}_n$ converges to the inner product $\langle\vec{p} \vec{q}\rangle$.
Hint: Using the following, apply the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality

$$
\langle\vec{x}, \vec{y}\rangle-\langle\vec{p}, \vec{q}\rangle=\langle\vec{x}-\vec{p}, \vec{y}-\vec{q}\rangle+\langle\vec{p}, \vec{y}-\vec{q}\rangle+\langle\vec{x}-\vec{p}, \vec{q}\rangle
$$
        

\end{Large}
\newpage

\begin{Large}
        Since {$\vec{x_n}$} converges to $  \vec{p} \to  \exists \epsilon >0  $ s.t 
        \[
                |\vec{x_n} - \vec{p} |< \epsilon
        .\] 
        let $\lambda \in \mathsbb{R}$\[
        \exists  \mid \lambda  \mid  \neq 0 \implies  \mid  \lambda  \mid  ( \mid \vec{x_n} - \vec{p} \mid  ) < |\lambda |\epsilon
        .\] 
        \[
                \implies | \lambda \vec{x_n}- \lambda \vec{p} | < \lambda \epsilon
        .\] 
        Now we consider the case $\lambda =0$, Then  $\forall \vec{x_k} \in \{\vec{x_n}\} $ we have $\lambda \vec{x_k}$ where  $\lambda =0 \implies 0 \vec{x_k}=\vec{0}$. Thus if $\lambda =0$  $\{\vec{x_n\}} = {\vec{0}}$.\\
        
        \vspace{200pt}
        We have the for $  \{\vec{x_n}\} $ converges to $ \vec{p} $. Thus by the same reasoning $  \{\vec{y_n}\} $  converges to $  \vec{q } $. Thus $ \forall k \in \mathbb{N}$, $ \vec{x_k} \to \vec{p}, \vec{y_k} \to \vec{q}$. Then if we pick any $\vec{y_k}+\vec{x_k} \to \vec{q}+\vec{p} = \{ \vec{z_n} \}$



        \vspace{200pt}\\
Cauchy swartz ineq: 
\[
       | <\vec{x}, \vec{y} >| \leq \| \vec{x}   \| \| \vec{y} \|
.\] 
\[

\langle\vec{x}, \vec{y}\rangle-\langle\vec{p}, \vec{q}\rangle=\langle\vec{x}-\vec{p}, \vec{y}-\vec{q}\rangle+\langle\vec{p}, \vec{y}-\vec{q}\rangle+\langle\vec{x}-\vec{p}, \vec{q}\rangle
.\] \\
Taking the magnitude:\\
\[
| \langle\vec{x}, \vec{y}\rangle-\langle\vec{p}, \vec{q}\rangle|=|\langle\vec{x}-\vec{p}, \vec{y}-\vec{q}\rangle+\langle\vec{p}, \vec{y}-\vec{q}\rangle+\langle\vec{x}-\vec{p}, \vec{q}\rangle|

.\] \\
\[
|\langle\vec{x}, \vec{y}\rangle-\langle\vec{p}, \vec{q}\rangle| = |\langle\vec{x}-\vec{p}, \vec{y}-\vec{q}\rangle+\langle\vec{p}, \vec{y}-\vec{q}\rangle+\langle\vec{x}-\vec{p}, \vec{q}\rangle|
\]
\[
 |\langle\vec{x}-\vec{p}, \vec{y}-\vec{q}\rangle+\langle\vec{p}, \vec{y}-\vec{q}\rangle+\langle\vec{x}-\vec{p}, \vec{q}\rangle| \leq  |\langle\vec{x}-\vec{p}, \vec{y}-\vec{q}\rangle|+|\langle\vec{p}, \vec{y}-\vec{q}\rangle|+|\langle\vec{x}-\vec{p}, \vec{q}\rangle| 
.\] 

\[
        \leq \| \vec{x}-\vec{p} \| \| \vec{y}-\vec{q} \|+ \|\vec{p} \| \|\vec{y}-\vec{q} \| + \|\vec{x}-\vec{p} \|\|\vec{q}\|
.\] 
We see each term converges as $\vec{x}$, $\vec{y}$ converge and $\vec{p}$,  $\vec{q}$ are finite. Thus the three terms converge to some finite $\alpha$, $\beta$, $\gamma$ respectivly. Thus we have:
\[
| \langle\vec{x}, \vec{y}\rangle-\langle\vec{p}, \vec{q}\rangle| \leq \alpha + \beta + \gamma = \epsilon
.\] So $\langle\vec{x}, \vec{y}\rangle-\langle\vec{p}, \vec{q}\rangle=$ converges.  \[
\implies \langle\vec{x}, \vec{y}\rangle \to  \langle\vec{p}, \vec{q}\rangle
.\] 
\end{Large}
