EExercise 2 Matrix norms (60pts)
In the following, you may use any standard facts from your Let $A: \mathbb{R}^m \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^s$ be a linear transformation represented by the $(s \times m)$-matrix $\left(A_{i, j}\right)_{\substack{1 \leq i \leq s \\ 1 \leq j \leq m}}$ with respect to the standard bases. We define the Hilbert-Schmidt norm of $T$ by

$$
\|A\|_{H S}:=\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^s \sum_{j=1}^m A_{i, j}^2}
$$

(15 pts) Show that, for any $\vec{x} \in \mathbb{R}^m$,

$$
\|A \vec{x}\| \leq\|A\|_{H S}\|\vec{x}\|
$$


Hint: what are the components of $A \vec{x}$ and how could we bound them using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality?
(15 pts) Show that, given linear transformations

$$
A: \mathbb{R}^m \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^s \text { and } B: \mathbb{R}^s \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^t
$$

the Hilbert Schmidt norm satisfies

$$
\|B A\|_{H S} \leq\|B\|_{H S}\|A\|_{H S}
$$

(15 pts) Denoting by $A^T$ the transpose of the matrix $A$, and by $\operatorname{tr}(M)$ the trace of a square matrix $M$, show that

$$
\|A\|_{H S}^2=\operatorname{tr}\left(A^T A\right)
$$

(15 pts) Let $A: \mathbb{R}^3 \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^3$ be given by the below matrix, and define a sequence of points in $\mathbb{R}^3$ by $\vec{x}_n:=A^{n-1} \vec{x}_1$, where $\vec{x}_1$ is given below. Prove that $\left\{\vec{x}_n\right\}$ converges to $\overrightarrow{0}$.

$$
A=\left(\begin{array}{ccc}
\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \frac{1}{2} \\
0 & \frac{1}{3} & \frac{1}{3} \\
\frac{1}{5} & \frac{1}{10} & 0
\end{array}\right), \quad \vec{x}_1=\left(\begin{array}{l}
1 \\
2 \\
3
\end{array}\right)
$$







\end{Large}
\newpage


\begin{Large}
        \textbf{2a}\\
        \\
        \[
                (A\vec{x})_i = \sum_{n=1}^{m} A_{i,j}x_j
        .\] 
        \[
                \|A\vec{x}\| = \sqrt{\sum_{n=i}^{s} (Ax)_i^2} 
        .\] \\
        Cauchy swartz implies:
        \[
         | <A_{i,j},x_j>|^2 \leq \|A_{i,j}\|^2 \|x_i\|^2
        .\] \[
        \|A \vec{x}\|  \leq \sqrt{\sum_{n=i}^{s} \left(\sum_{j=1}^{m} A_{i,j}\right)^2} \sqrt{\sum_{j=1}^{m} x_j^2} 
        .\] 
        \[
                \|A \vec{x}\| \leq \|A\|_{HS}\|\vec{x}\|
        .\] \\
        \vspace{200pt}\\
        \textbf{2b}\\
        We have \[
        \|BA\|_{HS}= \sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{t} \left(\sum_{q=1}^{m} \left(BA\right)_k^2\right)} 
        .\] \[
        |<B_{j,k} , A_{j,i}>| \leq \|A_i\|\|B_k\|

        .\]
\[
\|BA\|_{HS} \leq \sqrt{\sum_{k=1}^{t} \left( \sum_{j=1}^{s} B_j^2\right)} \sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{m} \left( \sum_{j=1}^{m} A_j^2\right)} 
.\] 
\[
\|BA\|_{HS} \leq \|B\|_{HS} \|A\|_{HS}
.\] 
\newpage
\textbf{2c}\\
\\
\[
\left( A^{T}A\right)_{i,k} = \sum_{j=1}^{s} A_{j,i}A_{j,k}
.\] 
\[
tr\left(A^{T}A\right) = \sum_{i=1}^{m} \left(A^{T}A\right)_{i,i}
.\] 
\[
= \sum_{i=1}^{m} \sum_{i=1}^{s} A_{i,i}^2
.\] 
And we have 
\[

\|A\|_{H S}^2=\sum_{i=1}^s \sum_{j=1}^m A_{i, j}^2
.\] Where $i$ and $j$ are just bookeeping indices. Thus we have \[
\|A\|_{HS}^2= tr\left( A^{T}A\right)
.\] as required.
\newpage
\\
\textbf{2d}\\
\\
Here I calculated the eigienvectors using mathematica to be (approximatly).
\\
$
\vec{x}_1=\left(\begin{array}{l}
2.879\\
 0.979\\
1
\end{array}\right), 
\vec{x}_2=\left(\begin{array}{l}
-3.669 \\
10.974\\
1
\end{array}\right), 
\vec{x}_3=\left(\begin{array}{l}
-0.0710\\
-0.0620\\
1
\end{array}\right), $
Thus we can diagonalise the matrix to get  $A=SJS^{-1}$. Explicitly we have:
\[
A= \begin{pmatrix}
-0.710 & -3.669 & 2.879\\
-0.620 & 10.974 & 0.979\\
1 & 1 & 1
\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}
-0.710 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 0.364 & 0\\
0 & 0 & .674
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}
-0.216 & -0.141 & 0.759\\
-0.035 & 0.077 & 0.024\\
0.250 & 0.064 & 0.674
\end{pmatrix}
.\] 
Now we can use the properties of diagonlaised matrices. ie $A^k= S J^{k}S^{-1}$, $\forall k>0$.
Here we see that we are rasing the $J$ matrix which consists of a diagonal with all entries $\leq 1$. We know that  $\forall p \in \mathbb{R} | \|p\|\leq 1$ as we take $p^{k}$ for large $k$ $p^{k}\to 0$. Thus we are multiplying each component by smaller and smaller numbers $J_i$, each $J_i\leq 1 \leq \epsilon$ for some finite $\epsilon$ . Since  $\vec{x_n}$ is finite and we multiply by a matrix which converges to $\vec{0}$ then the composition of  $\vec{x_n}= A^{n-1}\vec{x_1}$ must converge to $\vec{0}$.
\end{Large}

